{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'has soma location|expresses|capable of|projection|axon|target|marker|neuron' docs/relations_guide.md docs/patterns -g '*.md' -g '*.yaml'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/relations_guide.md:5:The aim of this document is to provide an accessible guide to how to use relations to record the properties that define cell types including location, lineage, function, morphology and marker genes. The term 'relations' here refers principally to OWL object properties, but also includes annoation properties used as shortcuts for more expressive logical axioms that can be programatically generated from them.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:50:[**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) applies in cases where an entire cell is within an anatomical structure, but some cells have parts in multiple anatomical structures. For example, many neurons span multiple regions of the central nervous system. The general relation for this is [**overlaps**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002131) (has some part in).\ndocs/relations_guide.md:52:[**overlaps**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002131) is not currently used directly in the cell ontology (time of writing 05/2023), but more specific relationships exist for recording the location of neurons and their parts. These are described in the next section.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:54:### Recording the location of neurons \ndocs/relations_guide.md:56:### [<u>has soma location</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:58:When neurobiologists talk about the location of vertebrate neurons, they are typically referring to soma location. The importance of soma location to identify is underscored by how commonly cell types are named, in part, by soma location. We therefore have a dedicated relation for recording this: [**'has soma location'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100).\ndocs/relations_guide.md:60:For example, [anterior horn motor neuron](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_2000048) has the following subclass axiom:\ndocs/relations_guide.md:62:[**'has soma location'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100) *some* ['ventral horn of spinal cord'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002257)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:64:**axiomatization of ‘has soma location’**\ndocs/relations_guide.md:68:-   *domain*: neuron \\# X has\\_soma\\_location some Y => X is inferred to be a subClassOf neuron\ndocs/relations_guide.md:70:-   *property chain*: has\\_soma\\_location o part\\_of --> has\\_soma\\_location \\# If x has soma location y and y is part\\_of z, then x has\\_soma\\_location\\_z\ndocs/relations_guide.md:74:'cortical interneuron' equivalentTo 'interneuron' that has\\_soma\\_location some 'cerebral cortex' \ndocs/relations_guide.md:76:'rosehip neuron' subClassOf interneuron  and has\\_soma\\_location some 'cortical layer 1' \ndocs/relations_guide.md:80:=> 'rosehip neuron' subClassOf 'cortical interneuron'\ndocs/relations_guide.md:84:A relationship between a neuron and a region, where the neuron has a functionally relevant number of output synapses in that region.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:86:'[<u>adult basket subesophageal neuron</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00051856)' SubClassOf [<u>sends synaptic output to region</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0013003)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:91:A relationship between a neuron and a region, where the neuron has a functionally relevant number of input synapses:\ndocs/relations_guide.md:93:e.g. '[<u>adult basket subesophageal neuron</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00051856)' SubClassOf ‘[<u>receives synaptic input in region</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0013002)*’\ndocs/relations_guide.md:98:Use this to record the tracts or nerves that a neuron’s projections fasciculate with.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:104:domain: neuron\ndocs/relations_guide.md:106:range: neuron projection bundle\ndocs/relations_guide.md:108:## Recording synaptic connectivity (neurons)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:110:To record neuron-to-neuron or motor neuron-to-target muscle connectivity, consider the following object properties. These properties should be used when connectivity is key to the definition, for example, in cases where a motor neuron type is defined by the type of muscle fiber on which it synapses.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:114:For example, ['alpha motor neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008038) SubClassOf [**synapsed to**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120) *some* ['extrafusal muscle fiber'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008046)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:120:For example, ['extrafusal muscle fiber'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008046) SubClassOf [**synapsed by**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002103) *some* ['alpha motor neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008038)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:124:Cellular function is recorded by linking GO biological process terms with the object properties [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) and ‘capable of part of’\ndocs/relations_guide.md:126:###  [<u>'capable of'</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:128:Use this relation where the cell is capable of carrying out the entirety of the process\ndocs/relations_guide.md:132:[**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) *some* ['androgen secretion'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0035935)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:134:### Recording neurotransmitter for neurons\ndocs/relations_guide.md:136:To record which neurotransmitter a neuron releases, use a [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) relation to link the neuron to a subclass of GO [neurotransmitter secretion](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0007269) that references a neurotransmitter type. This should be sufficient for autoclassification.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:138:e.g. ['medium spiny neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001474) [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) some ['gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0061534)\ndocs/relations_guide.md:140:### ‘capable of part of’\ndocs/relations_guide.md:144:e.g.  'retinal bipolar neuron' 'capable of part of' some 'visual perception'\ndocs/relations_guide.md:152:## Recording cell markers\ndocs/relations_guide.md:154:Only markers that are necessary to define a cell type should be recorded.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:156:### cell surface (protein) markers\ndocs/relations_guide.md:158:The cell ontology has a set of terms for recording cell surface markers.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:160:The most commonly used relation for recording markers is [**'has plasma\ndocs/relations_guide.md:162:object property is used to record cell surface markers, especially in\ndocs/relations_guide.md:180:Absence of a marker can be recorded using\ndocs/relations_guide.md:184:Warning - this is used in place of the more accurate OWL expression \"NOT has_part some X*** in order to keep within the EL profile of OWL. It's use with a general class as a target can potentially lead to reasoning errors.  \ndocs/relations_guide.md:186:### recording gene markers\ndocs/relations_guide.md:188:### ‘expresses’\ndocs/relations_guide.md:194:'lamp5 GABAergic cortical interneuron' EquivalentTo:\ndocs/relations_guide.md:195:interneuron and ('has soma location' some 'cerebral cortex')\ndocs/relations_guide.md:196:and ('capable of' some 'gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission') \ndocs/relations_guide.md:197:and (expresses some 'lysosome-associated membrane glycoprotein 5')\ndocs/relations_guide.md:234:neuronal), e.g.\ndocs/relations_guide.md:271:## Taxon constraints\ndocs/relations_guide.md:274:<https://oboacademy.github.io/obook/explanation/taxon-constraints-explainer/>.\ndocs/patterns/cellBearerOfQuality.md:32:| [CL:0000103](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000103) | bipolar neuron        | [CL:0000099](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000099) | interneuron  | [PATO:0070006](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0070006) | bipolar morphology       |\ndocs/patterns/cellBearerOfQuality.md:33:| [CL:4023077](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_4023077) | bitufted neuron       | [CL:0000099](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000099) | interneuron  | [PATO:0070012](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0070012) | bitufted cell morphology |\ndocs/patterns/taxonSpecific.md:1:# taxonSpecific \ndocs/patterns/taxonSpecific.md:3:[http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl/taxonSpecific](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl/taxonSpecific)\ndocs/patterns/taxonSpecific.md:6:A cell that is restricted to a specific taxon, such as CL:0001200 'lymphocyte of B lineage, CD19-positive' are only in mammals. Note - this is not to be used for any cell that is restricted to a taxon, this is for taxon-specific subclasses of existing cell types. This should hardly ever be used.\ndocs/patterns/taxonSpecific.md:12:{[cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000)}, {[taxon](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_1)}\ndocs/patterns/taxonSpecific.md:16:Any {[cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000)} that is part of a {[taxon](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_1)}.\ndocs/patterns/taxonSpecific.md:20:{[cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000)} and ([in taxon](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002162) some {[taxon](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_1)})\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:23:| CL:0000103 | bipolar neuron        | CL:0000099 | interneuron  | PATO:0070006 | bipolar morphology       |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:24:| CL:4023077 | bitufted neuron       | CL:0000099 | interneuron  | PATO:0070012 | bitufted cell morphology |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:27:### Cell capable of biological process\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:28:*Any cell that is involved in/capable of a particular biological process, such as acid secretion.*\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:45:| CL:0011005 | GABAergic interneuron                      | GO:0061534 | gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission | CL:0000099 | interneuron                     |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:46:| CL:0000617 | GABAergic neuron                           | GO:0061534 | gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission | CL:0000540 | neuron                          |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:83:### Taxon specific\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:84:*A cell that is restricted to a specific taxon, such as CL:0001200 'lymphocyte of B lineage, CD19-positive' are only in mammals. Note - this is not to be used for any cell that is restricted to a taxon, this is for taxon-specific subclasses of existing cell types. This should hardly ever be used.*\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:88:| IRI | http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl/taxonSpecific |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:89:| Name | taxonSpecific |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:90:| Classes | CL:0000000, NCBITaxon:1,  |\ndocs/patterns/overview.md:91:| Variables | cell (CL:0000000), taxon (NCBITaxon:1),  |\ndocs/patterns/cellCapableOfBiologicalProcess.md:6:Any cell that is involved in/capable of a particular biological process, such as acid secretion.\ndocs/patterns/cellCapableOfBiologicalProcess.md:13:A {[cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000)} that is capable of {[biological_process](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0008150)}.\ndocs/patterns/cellCapableOfBiologicalProcess.md:17:{[cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000000)} and ([capable of](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) some {[biological_process](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0008150)})\ndocs/patterns/cellCapableOfBiologicalProcess.md:25:| [CL:0011005](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0011005) | GABAergic interneuron                      | [GO:0061534](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0061534) | gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission | [CL:0000099](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000099) | interneuron                  